{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-119478",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165760/assets/ec6541d0566750d1057627a510020c572ff8a856cc5ff5e8d02d0aef0a783683.png)\n\nIn any ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is twice the length of the shorter leg.\n\nThe leg opposite the ${30}^{∘}$ angle is the shorter leg of the triangle. So here, the shorter leg has length $x=12.$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse must be\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 12=24.\n$$",
